id: braced
name: Braced
kind: conditions
maxStacks: 1
# Not a Combat Condition: nothing calls it one, and the Brace entry does not name it at
# all. The name is this system's, for a duration that has to be held somewhere.
combatCondition: no
source: Core Rule
description: >
  Set against the weather. Battle Weathers count a Tier lower until the start of your
  next turn - two Tiers with 4+ Ranks in Survival.
text: >
  Braced: the Brace Maneuver's mark. "Until the start of your next turn, treat all
  Battle Weathers as if they were 1 Weather Tier lower. If this would reduce the
  Weather Tier to 0, ignore the effects of that Battle Weather. If you have 4+ Skill
  Ranks in Survival, you may treat it as 2 Weather Tiers lower instead."

---

# --- The name is this system's, and that is worth saying outright ---------------------
#
# The Brace entry names no Condition. It gives a duration - "until the start of your next
# turn" - and something has to hold that, and a mark with a clock on it is what holds a
# duration here. So this file exists as machinery rather than as a rule, and the word
# "Braced" is not one a player will find in the rulebook.
#
# Not a Combat Condition, for the same reason Hyped and Analyzed are not: the entries that
# mean it say "gains the Impediment Combat Condition", and nothing here says anything of
# the sort. The checklist on the sheet stays the published eighteen.
#
# --- How many Tiers ---------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# "1 Weather Tier lower", and "2 ... instead" at 4+ Ranks in Survival. Both are read off
# the character as it stands rather than frozen when the Maneuver was used, which is the
# simpler of the two readings and the one that cannot go stale.
#
# The 4+ clause says "you MAY treat it as 2 instead", so this is a ceiling and not an
# order. What is written is what you are entitled to; taking less is the player's, and
# there is nothing here to stop them.
#
# Written as one and then another rather than as a choice between one and two, because an
# amount in this language holds no ternary - only a condition does. It reads better this
# way in any case: the Tier is the rule, and the Ranks buy a second one.
[passive]
weather.tiers += 1;
if (survival.ranks >= 4) {
  weather.tiers += 1;
}

# --- How it is applied -------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# `weather.tiers` is worked out ahead of everything else, in a phase of its own, and taken
# off the Weather Tier the character feels: every (WT) of the Weather, and every "Tier 2+",
# reads the Tier felt. "If this would reduce the Weather Tier to 0, ignore the effects of
# that Battle Weather": brought to 0, the Weather's effects are not gathered at all. Weather
# Resistant lowers the same Tier, and the two add up.
